  {"title":"Shreya Singhal v. Union of India (2015)","act_name":"IT Act 2000","section":"Section 66A","year":2015,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Struck down Section 66A IT Act criminalizing offensive online messages as unconstitutionally vague and violative of free speech.","judgement_text":"Section 66A is struck down as unconstitutional. Terms like offensive, menacing, annoying are vague and chill free speech.","keywords":"section 66a internet freedom free speech it act offensive message online censorship article 19"},
  {"title":"Joseph Shine v. Union of India (2018)","act_name":"IPC","section":"Section 497","year":2018,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Struck down Section 497 IPC adultery as unconstitutional. A woman's sexuality cannot be controlled by her husband.","judgement_text":"Section 497 is unconstitutional as it treats women as property of their husbands, violating Articles 14, 15, and 21.","keywords":"adultery section 497 ipc unconstitutional gender equality women rights marriage sexuality"},
  {"title":"Hussainara Khatoon v. State of Bihar (1979)","act_name":"CrPC Constitutional Law","section":"Article 21","year":1979,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"PIL exposing undertrial prisoners in Bihar jails for years without trial. Established right to speedy trial as fundamental right.","judgement_text":"Right to speedy trial is implicit in Article 21. Undertrial prisoners cannot stay in custody beyond maximum sentence period.","keywords":"speedy trial undertrial prisoners bail right to life article 21 prison judicial delay"},
  {"title":"Nandini Satpathy v. P.L. Dani (1978)","act_name":"CrPC Constitutional Law","section":"Article 20(3)","year":1978,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Expanded right against self-incrimination to pre-trial stages. Accused cannot be compelled to answer questions that might incriminate.","judgement_text":"Right against self-incrimination extends to investigation stages. Person cannot be compelled to witness against himself under Article 20(3).","keywords":"self incrimination article 20 right to silence accused investigation police questioning"},
  {"title":"State of West Bengal v. Anwar Ali Sarkar (1952)","act_name":"Constitution of India","section":"Article 14","year":1952,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Struck down West Bengal Special Courts Act as it allowed arbitrary classification of cases without reasonable nexus.","judgement_text":"Article 14 strikes at arbitrariness. Classification must be based on intelligible differentia having reasonable nexus to the object.","keywords":"equality article 14 reasonable classification arbitrary law discrimination special courts"},
  {"title":"MC Mehta v. Union of India (1987)","act_name":"Environmental Law","section":"Absolute Liability","year":1987,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Introduced doctrine of Absolute Liability. Enterprises in inherently dangerous activities are absolutely liable for harm regardless of negligence.","judgement_text":"Where an enterprise is engaged in hazardous activity and harm results, enterprise is absolutely liable. No exceptions unlike strict liability.","keywords":"absolute liability environmental pollution hazardous industry oleum gas leak compensation tort"},
  {"title":"Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973)","act_name":"Constitution of India","section":"Article 368","year":1973,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Landmark 13-judge bench ruling establishing Basic Structure Doctrine. Parliament cannot amend the basic structure of the Constitution.","judgement_text":"Parliament has constituent power under Article 368 but cannot destroy the basic structure of the Constitution including the rule of law.","keywords":"basic structure doctrine constitutional amendment article 368 parliament fundamental rights kesavananda"},
  {"title":"Navtej Singh Johar v. Union of India (2018)","act_name":"IPC","section":"Section 377","year":2018,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Decriminalized consensual same-sex relations between adults by reading down Section 377 IPC. Held homosexuality is natural and constitutional.","judgement_text":"Section 377 insofar as it criminalizes consensual same-sex conduct between adults is unconstitutional. Sexuality is a natural part of identity.","keywords":"section 377 homosexuality lgbtq gay consensual sexual orientation decriminalized rights dignity"},
  {"title":"ADM Jabalpur v. Shivkant Shukla (1976)","act_name":"Constitution of India","section":"Article 21","year":1976,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Emergency case where majority held habeas corpus not maintainable during Emergency. Later widely criticized as darkest hour of Indian judiciary.","judgement_text":"During national emergency, right to move courts for enforcement of fundamental rights under Articles 14, 21, 22 is suspended.","keywords":"emergency habeas corpus article 21 fundamental rights suspension ADM jabalpur internal emergency"},
  {"title":"Minerva Mills v. Union of India (1980)","act_name":"Constitution of India","section":"Article 368","year":1980,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Struck down 42nd Amendment that sought to give Parliament unlimited power to amend Constitution. Reaffirmed Basic Structure Doctrine.","judgement_text":"Parliament cannot grant itself unlimited amending power. Balance between fundamental rights and directive principles is part of basic structure.","keywords":"minerva mills basic structure 42nd amendment constitution parliament amending power directive principles"},
  {"title":"Golak Nath v. State of Punjab (1967)","act_name":"Constitution of India","section":"Article 368","year":1967,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Held Parliament cannot amend fundamental rights. Later overruled by Kesavananda Bharati but led to 24th Amendment.","judgement_text":"Fundamental rights cannot be abridged or taken away by Parliament through constitutional amendment. They are transcendental and inalienable.","keywords":"golak nath fundamental rights amendment parliament property right article 13 368"},
  {"title":"Romesh Thappar v. State of Madras (1950)","act_name":"Constitution of India","section":"Article 19","year":1950,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Held ban on a communist journal violated freedom of speech under Article 19. Established press freedom as fundamental right.","judgement_text":"Freedom of speech includes freedom of press. Banning a publication without reasonable restriction amounts to unconstitutional censorship.","keywords":"press freedom free speech article 19 censorship publication communist media restriction fundamental right"},

  # ── Criminal Law ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  {"title":"Bachan Singh v. State of Punjab (1980)","act_name":"IPC","section":"IPC 302","year":1980,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Upheld constitutional validity of death penalty but restricted it to rarest of rare cases. Laid down guidelines for capital punishment.","judgement_text":"Death penalty is constitutionally valid but should be imposed only in rarest of rare cases after considering both crime and criminal circumstances.","keywords":"death penalty rarest of rare capital punishment murder section 302 ipc sentencing guidelines"},
  {"title":"Machhi Singh v. State of Punjab (1983)","act_name":"IPC","section":"IPC 302","year":1983,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Elaborated rarest of rare doctrine for death penalty. Laid down five categories of cases where death penalty may be appropriate.","judgement_text":"Five categories: manner of commission is extremely brutal; motive shows extreme depravity; magnitude is staggering; victim is innocent; anti-social or socially abhorrent nature.","keywords":"rarest of rare death penalty murder categories brutal massacre capital punishment ipc 302"},
  {"title":"State of Rajasthan v. Balchand (1977)","act_name":"CrPC","section":"Section 437","year":1977,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Bail is the rule, jail is the exception. Accused should ordinarily be released on bail especially in cases of minor offences.","judgement_text":"The basic rule is bail not jail. Courts granting bail should consider gravity of accusation, character and antecedents of accused.","keywords":"bail jail rule exception accused release crpc section 437 438 criminal procedure bond"},
  {"title":"Joginder Kumar v. State of U.P. (1994)","act_name":"CrPC","section":"Section 41","year":1994,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Set guidelines for arrest by police. Arrest cannot be made mechanically. Reasons must be recorded for arrest.","judgement_text":"No arrest should be made only because it is lawful. The existence of power to arrest is one thing, justification for arrest is another.","keywords":"arrest police power guidelines section 41 crpc justification rights of accused informing family"},
  {"title":"D.K. Basu v. State of West Bengal (1997)","act_name":"Constitutional Law / CrPC","section":"Article 21","year":1997,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Issued landmark guidelines to prevent custodial torture and death. Police must follow procedural safeguards during arrest.","judgement_text":"Custodial death is perhaps one of the worst crimes. Eleven guidelines must be followed during arrest including medical examination and right to inform.","keywords":"custodial torture death arrest guidelines police DK basu article 21 fundamental rights lockup"},
  {"title":"Mohd. Ahmed Khan v. Shah Bano Begum (1985)","act_name":"Muslim Personal Law","section":"CrPC 125","year":1985,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Muslim divorced wife entitled to maintenance under Section 125 CrPC. A divorced Muslim woman can claim maintenance beyond iddat period.","judgement_text":"Section 125 CrPC applies to all citizens regardless of religion. A divorced Muslim wife is entitled to maintenance to prevent destitution.","keywords":"shah bano maintenance divorce muslim personal law crpc 125 alimony religious neutrality secular law"},
  {"title":"State v. Delhi High Court Bar Assn. (1998)","act_name":"Constitution / CrPC","section":"Article 21","year":1998,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Examined conditions of undertrial prisoners and right to legal aid. Free legal aid is a constitutional requirement under Article 21.","judgement_text":"Access to justice is part of constitutional right to life. State must provide free legal aid to accused who cannot afford counsel.","keywords":"legal aid free lawyer undertrial prisoner article 21 access to justice constitutional right counsel"},
  {"title":"Zahira Habibullah Sheikh v. State of Gujarat (2004)","act_name":"CrPC Evidence Act","section":"Best Bakery Case","year":2004,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Best Bakery case. Witnesses turned hostile under threat. SC transferred case outside Gujarat to ensure fair trial.","judgement_text":"A fair trial is the heart of criminal jurisprudence. Witness protection and fair trial are non-negotiable constitutional imperatives.","keywords":"best bakery gujarat riots witness hostile fair trial transfer case protection threat communal violence"},
  {"title":"Nar Singh Pal v. Union of India (2000)","act_name":"Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances Act","section":"NDPS Act","year":2000,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Examined reverse burden of proof under NDPS Act. Once prosecution establishes possession, burden shifts to accused to prove innocence.","judgement_text":"In NDPS offences, once the prosecution establishes recovery and possession, burden of proof shifts to the accused under the Act.","keywords":"ndps drugs narcotics burden of proof reverse onus possession recovery accused bail stringent"},
  {"title":"Suman Rani v. State of Haryana (2006)","act_name":"IPC","section":"IPC 307 326","year":2006,"court":"Punjab and Haryana HC","summary":"Acid attack case establishing precedent for enhanced compensation and mandatory minimum sentence for acid attack victims.","judgement_text":"Acid attack is a heinous crime causing permanent disability and disfigurement. Maximum punishment with compensation to victim is appropriate.","keywords":"acid attack victim compensation ipc 326a disfigurement punishment enhanced women harassment"},
  {"title":"Paramvir Singh Saini v. Baljit Singh (2020)","act_name":"CrPC","section":"Section 41","year":2020,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Directed installation of CCTV cameras in all police stations to prevent custodial torture and ensure accountability.","judgement_text":"CCTV cameras must be installed in all police stations to record every activity. Failure is failure of constitutional duty.","keywords":"cctv police station custodial torture accountability section 41 fundamental rights human rights article 21"},
  {"title":"Nilabati Behera v. State of Orissa (1993)","act_name":"Constitutional Law","section":"Article 21","year":1993,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"State is liable to pay compensation for custodial death. Awarded compensation to mother for son who died in police custody.","judgement_text":"State is liable under public law for depriving person of right to life. Compensation is mandatory for custodial death.","keywords":"custodial death compensation state liability article 21 police custody human rights mother writ"},
  {"title":"Common Cause (A Regd. Society) v. Union of India (2018)","act_name":"Constitution of India","section":"Article 21","year":2018,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Recognized right to die with dignity as fundamental right. Allowed passive euthanasia and advance medical directives.","judgement_text":"Right to die with dignity is a fundamental right. A person has the right to execute an advance directive (living will) for end of life care.","keywords":"right to die dignity passive euthanasia living will advance directive article 21 terminal illness mercy"},
  {"title":"State of Maharashtra v. Madhkar Narayan (1991)","act_name":"IPC","section":"IPC 376","year":1991,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Held rape of a woman of easy virtue is still rape. The character of a woman cannot justify rape or reduce the sentence.","judgement_text":"Even a woman of easy virtue has a right to her body. No man can outrage her modesty or rape her. Character evidence is irrelevant in rape trial.","keywords":"rape character woman victim section 376 ipc easy virtue prior sexual history consent irrelevant"},
  {"title":"Bodhisattwa Gautam v. Subhra Chakraborty (1996)","act_name":"IPC","section":"IPC 376 376A","year":1996,"court":"Supreme Court of India","summary":"Rape is not just physical assault but is an act of aggression against dignity of woman. Interim compensation can be awarded by court.","judgement_text":"Rape is violation of fundamental rights under Article 21. Courts can award interim compensation to victim before conviction.","keywords":"rape compensation victim interim relief dignity article 21 sexual assault women rights prosecution"},

